1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,880
In today’s astronomy lecture, I’m going to talk about the need for a system

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:11,560
to manage the movement of satellites and other objects in orbit around the Earth.

3
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,780
In other words, a Space Traffic Management system.

4
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:22,327
We already have effective Air Traffic Control systems that are used internationally to ensure

5
00:00:22,327 --> 00:00:25,660
that planes navigate our skies safely.

6
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:29,359
Well, Space Traffic Management is a similar concept,

7
00:00:29,359 --> 00:00:33,300
but focusing on the control of satellites.

8
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:38,116
The aim of such a system would be to prevent the danger of collisions

9
00:00:38,116 --> 00:00:42,490
in space between the objects in orbit around the Earth.

10
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:47,286
In order to do this, we’d need to have a set of legal

11
00:00:47,286 --> 00:00:48,068
measures,

12
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:54,770
and we’d also have to develop the technical systems to enable us to prevent

13
00:00:54,770 --> 00:00:54,820
such accidents.

14
00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:02,470
But unfortunately, at present we don’t actually have a Space Traffic Management system that

15
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:02,520
works.

16
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:04,383
So why not?

17
00:01:04,383 --> 00:01:08,470
What are the problems in developing such a system?

18
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:14,171
Well, for one thing, satellites are relatively cheap these days, compared with how they

19
00:01:14,171 --> 00:01:19,873
were in the past, meaning that more people can afford to put them into

20
00:01:19,873 --> 00:01:20,280
space.

21
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:24,487
So there’s a lot more of them out there,

22
00:01:24,487 --> 00:01:30,797
and people aren’t just launching single satellites but whole constellations, consisting of thousands of

23
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:32,900
them designed to work together.

24
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:37,340
So space is getting more crowded every day.

25
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:42,696
But in spite of this, one thing you may be surprised to learn is

26
00:01:42,696 --> 00:01:48,434
that you can launch a satellite into space and, once it’s out there, it

27
00:01:48,434 --> 00:01:53,790
doesn’t have to send back any information to Earth to allow its identification.

28
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:59,162
So while we have international systems for ensuring we know where the planes in

29
00:01:59,162 --> 00:02:00,314
our skies are,

30
00:02:00,314 --> 00:02:03,768
and to prevent them from colliding with one another,

31
00:02:03,768 --> 00:02:09,524
when it comes to the safety of satellites, at present we don’t have anything

32
00:02:09,524 --> 00:02:12,210
like enough proper ways of tracking them.

33
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:16,620
And it isn’t just entire satellites that we need to consider.

34
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:22,438
A greater threat is the huge amount of space debris in orbit around the

35
00:02:22,438 --> 00:02:27,840
Earth – broken bits of satellite and junk from space stations and so on.

36
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:33,325
And some of these are so small that they can be very hard to

37
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:33,717
identify,

38
00:02:33,717 --> 00:02:36,460
but they can still be very dangerous.

39
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:43,622
In addition, some operators may be unwilling to share information about the satellites they’ve

40
00:02:43,622 --> 00:02:44,100
launched.

41
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:48,306
For example, a satellite may be designed for military purposes,

42
00:02:48,306 --> 00:02:52,092
or it may have been launched for commercial reasons,

43
00:02:52,092 --> 00:02:57,140
and the operators don’t want competitors to have information about it.

44
00:02:57,140 --> 00:03:03,546
And even if the operators are willing to provide it, the information isn’t easy

45
00:03:03,546 --> 00:03:04,400
to collect.

46
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:11,252
Details are needed about the object itself, as well as about its location at a particular time –

47
00:03:11,252 --> 00:03:14,880
and remember that a satellite isn’t very big,

48
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:20,120
and it’s likely to be moving at thousands of kilometres an hour.

49
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:27,692
We don’t have any sensors that can constantly follow something moving so fast,

50
00:03:27,692 --> 00:03:35,265
so all that the scientists can do is to put forward a prediction concerning

51
00:03:35,265 --> 00:03:38,510
where the satellite is heading next.

52
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:43,320
So those are some of the problems that we’re facing.

53
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:48,190
Let’s consider now some of the solutions that have been suggested.

54
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:53,480
One key issue is the way in which information is dealt with.

55
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,107
We need more information,

56
00:03:55,107 --> 00:03:59,581
but it also needs to be accessible at a global level,

57
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:05,276
so we need to establish shared standards that we can all agree on for

58
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:08,530
the way in which this information is presented.

59
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:12,284
We already do this in other areas of science,

60
00:04:12,284 --> 00:04:17,290
so although this is a challenge, it’s not an impossible task.

61
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:22,449
Then, as all this information’s collected, it needs to be put together

62
00:04:22,449 --> 00:04:24,434
so it can be used,

63
00:04:24,434 --> 00:04:29,990
and that will involve creating a single database on which it can be entered.

64
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:36,562
As we continue to push forward new developments, congestion of the space environment is

65
00:04:36,562 --> 00:04:38,440
only going to increase.

66
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:44,096
To cope with this, we need to develop a system like the one I’ve

67
00:04:44,096 --> 00:04:47,866
described to coordinate the work of the numerous spacecraft operators,

68
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:53,522
but it’s also essential that this system is one that establishes trust in the

69
00:04:53,522 --> 00:04:57,670
people that use it, both nationally and at a global level.

70
00:04:57,670 --> 00:05:01,320
One interesting development …
